als, and a lobby where visitors learn how quickly an arrival becomes public news.","category":"hotel","location_type":"venue","sort_order":60,"depth":1,"visual_asset_key":"calders-run-calder-house-hotel","hours":{"open":"00:00","close":"24:00"},"possible_activities":["lodging","supper","balcony conversation","arrival assistance"],"access_metadata":{"mode":"public_frontage","initiallyDiscoverable":true,"requiredState":null,"privateAreasRequireInvitation":true,"publicMapVisible":true},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","district":"main-street","assetStatus":"pending","photoStatus":"pending","userLocalClock":true,"publicMapVisible":true},"canonical_visual_context":{"canonicalPrompt":"Fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. Calder House Hotel: A three-story brick hotel with a shaded balcony, dependable meals, and a lobby where visitors learn how quickly an arrival becomes public news. Show brick balcony, green shutters, worn register, courtyard washstand. The front desk faces the street doors. A public dining room opens to a side courtyard; a narrow service stair reaches staff rooms. Guest corridors and the register behind the desk are private. A single coherent, inhabited architectural scene; period materials, natural light, no text or logo. Compose around this location’s own architecture, furnishings and landscape. Keep the railway bridge outside the frame; windows show nearby buildings, vegetation or sky.","visualAnchors":["brick balcony","green shutters","worn register","courtyard washstand"],"avoid":["modern objects","readable private papers","railway bridge in background","repeated town panorama"],"railwayBridgeVisible":false},"canonical_lore":{"version":2,"authored":true,"summary":"A three-story brick hotel with a shaded balcony, dependable meals, and a lobby where visitors learn how quickly an arrival becomes public news.","publicHistory":["The hotel absorbed two older boarding buildings after the 1872 flood. Its respectable front conceals mismatched