house occupies an expanded reading-room building, and its piecemeal construction is visible from the alley.","sensory":["warm brick after sunset","horse tack and fresh bread","press machinery behind an open window"],"rhythm":"Freight and shopping dominate morning; court business and repairs fill afternoon; hotel balconies and supper visits soften the evening.","fault":"Public frontage suggests stability while rents and credit determine who can remain behind it.","ritual":"Shop staff exchange small change and errands before the first coach arrival.","weather":"Dust gathers in doorways; runoff makes the lower steps slippery after rain.","story":"A disputed public notice can connect the press, courthouse, marshal, and hotel without any one worker knowing the whole story.","imagePrompt":"Historical Western town, fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. The west-bank commercial terrace, where the town puts on its public face and conducts the business everyone eventually needs. Visible details: warm brick after sunset, horse tack and fresh bread, press machinery behind an open window. An elevated view along a lively west-bank street toward a green river, the unfinished bridge, and an east-bank locomotive. Red bluffs define the horizon. The town contains working yards, planted courtyards, painted storefronts, ordinary pedestrians, and warm rooms visible at dusk. One coherent scene focused on this district; no labels or text."},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:604a7a05-0d7e-5ea2-9357-1e9953bf3bd1","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"id":"604a7a05-0d7e-5ea2-9357-1e9953bf3bd1","authoringNumber":2,"worldId":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","slug":"lantern-row","name":"Lantern Row","nodeType":"district","parentLocationId":null,"summary":"The evening district, with music, good food, paid companionship, bathing, cards, and people whose labor lets others relax.","history":"Temporary drinking rooms gave way to purpose-built businesses when miners began receiving regular pay. Adult establishments and theaters funded improvements that more respectable neighbors also use.","sensory":["piano heard through two different walls","lamp oil and kitchen onions","washed linen in an upstairs corridor"],"rhythm":"Late mornings belong to staff and deliveries. Rehearsal and preparation grow into crowded evenings; closing-time meals create their own community.","fault":"The people who attract customers may have the least control over leases and working conditions.","ritual":"After a difficult night, staff send a plate from the Night Kitchen to the business still closing.","weather":"Open windows cool crowded rooms; storms trap strangers together and dampen musical instruments.","story":"A changed lease, a touring offer, and a lucrative patron divide people who usually protect one another.","imagePrompt":"Historical Western town, fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. The evening district, with music, good food, paid companionship, bathing, cards, and people whose labor lets others relax. Visible details: piano heard through two different walls, lamp oil and kitchen onions, washed linen in an upstairs corridor. An elevated view along a lively west-bank street toward a green river, the unfinished bridge, and an east-bank locomotive. Red bluffs define the horizon. The town contains working yards, planted courtyards, painted storefronts, ordinary pedestrians, and warm rooms visible at dusk. One coherent scene focused on this district; no labels or text."},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:4331f47a-a84a-5a9c-84bb-29e462d61985","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"id":"4331f47a-a84a-5a9c-84bb-29e462d61985","authoringNumber":3,"worldId":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","slug":"river-ward","name":"River Ward","nodeType":"district","parentLocationId":null,"summary":"The old working slope to the river, full of shared services, boarding rooms, small trades, and accumulated memory.","history":"The flood erased several streets but not their households. Rebuilt walls contain salvaged material; the headworks and ferry compact are part of family history.","sensory":["damp rope","soap and sun-dried cloth","food carried down a boardinghouse stair"],"rhythm":"Early departures, clinic and market business, afternoon repairs, and communal supper create frequent encounters across occupations.","fault":"Improvement schemes describe the ward as inefficient while depending on its cheap rooms and labor.","ritual":"People leave dry boards near doors for arrivals with wet boots.","weather":"High water directly changes work and travel; summer shade makes the ward a refuge from hotter streets.","story":"A delayed ferry, unpaid wages, and a shortage at the clinic reveal whose needs a new transport system actually serves.","imagePrompt":"Historical Western town, fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. The old working slope to the river, full of shared services, boarding rooms, small trades, and accumulated memory. Visible details: damp rope, soap and sun-dried cloth, food carried down a boardinghouse stair. An elevated view along a lively west-bank street toward a green river, the unfinished bridge, and an east-bank locomotive. Red bluffs define the horizon. The town contains working yards, planted courtyards, painted storefronts, ordinary pedestrians, and warm rooms visible at dusk. One coherent scene focused on this district; no labels or text."},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:b825b72f-5bd8-5f53-a5a3-e4b862932ae7","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"id":"b825b72f-5bd8-5f53-a5a3-e4b862932ae7","authoringNumber":4,"worldId":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","slug":"the-railhead","name":"The Railhead","nodeType":"district","parentLocationId":null,"summary":"The east-bank terminus, where temporary structures, demanding work, and speculative confidence compete to define the future.","history":"Last year’s survey camp is already a depot, supply street, office compound, and growing settlement. Its residents resent being treated as visitors to the west-bank town.","sensory":["coal smoke","fresh-cut timber","telegraph clicks through a thin wall"],"rhythm":"Dispatch and deliveries begin early. Survey and bridge crews move through daylight; correspondence and repairs continue after passenger activity slows.","fault":"The railway speaks as one institution while its employees have different wages, risks, and long-term prospects.","ritual":"The day’s first passenger arrival is checked from the provisioner’s porch with mugs in hand.","weather":"Wind exposes temporary buildings; high water can strand workers away from friends and appointments.","story":"An alternative survey route forces the people building the bridge to ask who gets to decide what progress costs.","imagePrompt":"Historical Western town, fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. The east-bank terminus, where temporary structures, demanding work, and speculative confidence compete to define the future. Visible details: coal smoke, fresh-cut timber, telegraph clicks through a thin wall. An elevated view along a lively west-bank street toward a green river, the unfinished bridge, and an east-bank locomotive. Red bluffs define the horizon. The town contains working yards, planted courtyards, painted storefronts, ordinary pedestrians, and warm rooms visible at dusk. One coherent scene focused on this district; no labels or text."},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:cff3d4a7-0a30-57e0-ba3f-d74db9b72810","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"id":"cff3d4a7-0a30-57e0-ba3f-d74db9b72810","authoringNumber":5,"worldId":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","slug":"cottonwood-valley","name":"Cottonwood Valley","nodeType":"district","parentLocationId":null,"summary":"The cultivated river corridor, with orchards, grazing, workshops, family homes, and disagreements that travel through irrigation channels.","history":"Several patterns of cultivation precede the modern town. The water register overlays older practices imperfectly, and later ranch consolidation has never settled every claim.","sensory":["cut grass beside dry earth","milk pails and shade cloth","moving water behind a locked gate"],"rhythm":"Animals and irrigation require early work; afternoons shift toward repair and household business; evening brings adult lessons, porch visits, and shared meals.","fault":"Local independence can conceal dependence on a neighbor’s gate, money, or permission.","ritual":"At the first cool evening after a hot spell, households bring food to the meeting-house yard.","weather":"Heat changes tasks and swimming conditions; a distant storm can alter river access without rain falling locally.","story":"A night water diversion can be an act of desperation, advantage, or both, and solving it does not by itself settle allocation.","imagePrompt":"Historical Western town, fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. The cultivated river corridor, with orchards, grazing, workshops, family homes, and disagreements that travel through irrigation channels. Visible details: cut grass beside dry earth, milk pails and shade cloth, moving water behind a locked gate. An elevated view along a lively west-bank street toward a green river, the unfinished bridge, and an east-bank locomotive. Red bluffs define the horizon. The town contains working yards, planted courtyards, painted storefronts, ordinary pedestrians, and warm rooms visible at dusk. One coherent scene focused on this district; no labels or text."},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:4aa276e2-1323-53a9-b9eb-d8de7cf3547e","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"id":"4aa276e2-1323-53a9-b9eb-d8de7cf3547e","authoringNumber":6,"worldId":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","slug":"cinder-bluffs","name":"Cinder Bluffs","nodeType":"district","parentLocationId":null,"summary":"The southern mining country and old road, with small claims, industrial workings, relay stations, and communities beyond easy supervision.","history":"Commercial silver connected scattered routes and camps. The 1881 collapse left a mutual society, a cemetery expansion, and unresolved arguments about responsibility.","sensory":["ore dust on cuffs","mules shifting under shade","wind through an abandoned loading frame"],"rhythm":"Mining and travel start early. Supply exchanges fill midday; late arrivals gather at stations and trading rooms before the roads become difficult.","fault":"Independence often depends on credit, an accurate assay, a working animal, or someone willing to carry a message.","ritual":"A returned traveler is offered water before being asked for news.","weather":"Exposed routes become slow in heat and unsafe during sudden runoff; night travel requires an authored purpose.","story":"A borrowed horse and disputed mineral sample can link the livery, couriers, miners, and distant households.","imagePrompt":"Historical Western town, fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. The southern mining country and old road, with small claims, industrial workings, relay stations, and communities beyond easy supervision. Visible details: ore dust on cuffs, mules shifting under shade, wind through an abandoned loading frame. An elevated view along a lively west-bank street toward a green river, the unfinished bridge, and an east-bank locomotive. Red bluffs define the horizon. The town contains working yards, planted courtyards, painted storefronts, ordinary pedestrians, and warm rooms visible at dusk. One coherent scene focused on this district; no labels or text."},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:6091676c-6511-5aa5-81a4-6582b38dbc30","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"id":"6091676c-6511-5aa5-81a4-6582b38dbc30","authoringNumber":1,"worldId":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","slug":"calder-house-hotel","name":"Calder House Hotel","nodeType":"place","parentLocationId":"8ea973d5-94fe-5c5c-8cb3-84a90abef76e","category":"hotel","publicSummary":"A three-story brick hotel with a shaded balcony, dependable meals, and a lobby where visitors learn how quickly an arrival becomes public news.","history":"The hotel absorbed two older boarding buildings after the 1872 flood. Its respectable front conceals mismatched floors and an inexpensive staff wing. The current management rents rooms to railway representatives, traveling performers, and locals between leases.","layout":"The front desk faces the street doors. A public dining room opens to a side courtyard; a narrow service stair reaches staff rooms. Guest corridors and the register behind the desk are private.","socialTexture":"Breakfast brings departures and account disputes. Afternoons belong to letters and quiet waiting. Evening balconies host conversation, while Rafael controls the practical business of late arrivals.","authorOnlyPrivateTruth":"Rafael has quietly reserved one low-priced room for stranded travelers using gratuities of his own. Management has not authorized a permanent relief arrangement.","storySeeds":["An investor wants a familiar tenant displaced before a private meeting.","A room reservation becomes a choice between a profitable guest and someone with nowhere to go."],"activities":["lodging","supper","balcony conversation","arrival assistance"],"visualAnchors":["brick balcony","green shutters","worn register","courtyard washstand"],"etiquette":"A guest name is not public entertainment. Ask before entering a corridor or joining someone on a balcony. Staff assistance does not purchase private attention.","primaryCharacterIds":["a71259b6-01ca-57c1-b8f6-1f4a2ed5d8d3"],"regularCharacterIds":["a71259b6-01ca-57c1-b8f6-1f4a2ed5d8d3","63a4bf14-598c-509f-861f-46a1ce3c729b","77ae229b-0456-55e9-bf5a-6358ef347bcc"],"visitorHours":{"opens":"00:00","closes":"24:00","overnight":false,"timezone":"user_local"},"access":{"mode":"public_frontage","initiallyDiscoverable":true,"requiredState":null,"privateAreasRequireInvitation":true},"imagePrompt":"Fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. Calder House Hotel: A three-story brick hotel with a shaded balcony, dependable meals, and a lobby where visitors learn how quickly an arrival becomes public news. Show brick balcony, green shutters, worn register, courtyard washstand. The front desk faces the street doors. A public dining room opens to a side courtyard; a narrow service stair reaches staff rooms. Guest corridors and the register behind the desk are private. A single coherent, inhabited architectural scene; period materials, natural light, no text or logo.","asset":{"key":"calders-run-calder-house-hotel","status":"not_generated"}},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:b8e6063d-69c1-5791-bb0d-595e8ea26148","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"i