abor.","layout":"A public observation rail sits away from active construction. Material yards, masonry, rigging, and the unfinished span are restricted. The bridge is not a usable crossing at baseline.","socialTexture":"Work follows daylight and conditions. Breaks gather tired crews with little patience for theatrical risk. Tom’s assigned jobs change as the project advances.","authorOnlyPrivateTruth":"A delivery substitution has reduced the reliability of a temporary work platform. Tom documented his concern; establishing responsibility requires purchasing records and inspection.","storySeeds":["A young worker decides how to insist on a concern being heard.","Visitors argue about the project while overlooking the people building it."],"activities":["observing construction","authorized deliveries","crew conversations on breaks","public project discussion"],"visualAnchors":["unfinished span","stacked stone","timber staging","crew water barrels"],"etiquette":"Observation space is public; active works are not. A story should reward reporting concerns and respecting skilled work, not invent procedural stunt instructions.","primaryCharacterIds":["e35b15cf-fcb2-5955-829c-3cb37661c7ac"],"regularCharacterIds":["e35b15cf-fcb2-5955-829c-3cb37661c7ac","27a5c487-a218-52b9-8798-2906889b7faa","dcd714c0-b3e9-5baf-9741-576729534661"],"visitorHours":{"opens":"06:00","closes":"19:00","overnight":false,"timezone":"user_local"},"access":{"mode":"public_frontage","initiallyDiscoverable":true,"requiredState":null,"privateAreasRequireInvitation":true},"imagePrompt":"Fictional territorial Southwest, 1888. Bridge Works: The unfinished bridge is a visible argument about the future, made from demanding work rather than the confident language of a prospectus. Show unfinished span, stacked stone, timber staging, crew water barrels. A public observation rail sits away from active construction. Material yards, masonry, rigging, and the unfinished span are restricted. The bridge is not a usable crossing at baseline. A single coherent, inhabited architectural scene; period materials, natural light, no text or logo.","asset":{"key":"calders-run-bridge-works","status":"not_generated"}},"metadata":{"source":"calders_run_authoring_v1","authoringSchema":"calders-run-authoring-pack-v1"}},{"world_id":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","content_key":"location:ec896c98-a5c1-5cdc-b6e9-96cdefc12925","content_type":"location_canon","knowledge_scope":"story","content_level":"standard","payload":{"id":"ec896c98-a5c1-5cdc-b6e9-96cdefc12925","authoringNumber":29,"worldId":"31740169-035e-5b10-8c9d-98b206e9f24b","slug":"railroad-freight-office","name":"Railroad Freight Office","nodeType":"place","parentLocationId":"b825b72f-5bd8-5f53-a5a3-e4b862932ae7","category":"freight and land office","publicSummary":"The railway’s local office combines ordinary freight service with land negotiations whose consequences extend well beyond the building.","history":"Halden secured a substantial office before the station had reliable shelter. Its furniture and punctual correspondence deliberately communicate permanence.","layout":"A public counter handles freight business. Appointments lead to a meeting room. Contracts, investor correspondence, and staff records remain private in locked offices.","socialTexture":"Mornings are transactional, afternoons negotiated. Evening appointments may be socially polished but remain business unless the participants explicitly choose otherwise.","authorOnlyPrivateTruth":"Halden’s authority to approve the alternative route is narrower than he implies. He needs investor consent and fears losing his local position if he cannot obtain it.","storySeeds":["A negotiator discovers the agent also has a superior to answer to.","A helpful concession is tied to an exclusion hidden elsewhere in the draft."],"activities":["freight booking","business appointments","contract discussion","public information requests"],"visualAnchors":["large route map","new oak desk","fre